I obtained a copy of Nethack 3.1.3 for DOS and according to one of the
readme files it is supposed to come with a .pif and .ico as well as be
"windows aware."

I played the game under windows  a few times  and each time it unceremoniosly
(without warning) exited DOS and kicked me back to windows (I lost one game
this way although I believe the game to be "recoverable" ).  There was no
pif included and it is possible I was not allocating enough memory or 
priority (I am not much of DOS and am even newer to Windows).
I never got passed the 2nd level without being kicked to MS-windows.

Has anyone experienced something similar?  Does anyone have any suggestions for
PIF settings?  Should I not bother with 3.1.3? (haven't notice any benefits yet
but I have not even reached the Gnomish mines).  What is the story on 3.1.3?


Not related note:
3 months or so ago I posted a note about a "blackhole" bug I experienced in
3.1.1 (where I "sucked" an entire levels contents into my backed while jumping
into trapdoor where I could not move because of all the boulders, chests, and
rocks I was carrying -- also the game crashed shortly thereafter) and stopped
reading this newsgroup so I was wondering if anyone knows about any discussion
or similar bug reports.

And if you are still reading, a completely tangential note:

I started playing Nethack at version 3.0.j and after many struggles learned
the game, tricks, spoilers, and the like and completed it several times
before becoming bored with it.  3.1/3.1.1 renewed my interest and with my 
previous experience and a bit of luck I finished the game on my first try.
At this point I vowed to play the game up to the castle with each character
(I think the game gets boring in the tunnels for the most part) without any
"saving/restoring" or wishing or "Elbereth" or polymorphing objects.  I mention
 this only because the game becomes very, very hard without these properties
I "arbitrarily" decided were not part of how I wanted to play it.  I do not
suggest that others play the game this way, or the game be written in any
way to accomodate those who wish to play this way.  I only add this note
because I have occaisionally seen notes about playing the game in a more
"pure" fashion (I got the idea from a post in this newsgroup) but would like
to add that it is A LOT harder than it sounds.  Trust me.





